      "note": "The root box is a real word with a defensible gloss, but the arrow is genealogically wrong in the way Nourai's own reference already warns about. Horn no. 948 (p. 211 — exactly the page cited) reads: 'göher \"Edelstein; Materie, Ursprung, Geschlecht\", arab. LW. jauhar \"Edelstein; Substanz\". skr. gōtra- \"Geschlecht, Ursprung\"; phlv. göhr bezw. göhar \"Materie, Substanz\". Die Bedeutung \"Edelstein\" ist erst sekundär.' Horn adduces Sanskrit gōtra- as a comparandum (cognate), never as the source of the Iranian word — Sanskrit is not an ancestor of Middle Persian. Hübschmann, Persische Studien p. 96, in his numbered note to precisely this Horn paragraph, goes further: '948. Die Etymologie ist bedenklich, da gotrá- im Veda \"Kuhstall\" bedeutet und im Np. *gōhr (nicht gōhar) lauten sollte.' Modern reconstruction resolves both objections: Middle Persian gwhl /gōhr/ goes back to Old Persian *gauθrā, itself from Proto-Iranian *gawθraH 'jewel', beside *gawθram 'family, lineage', an exact formal match to Sanskrit gotra- (*gaw- 'cow' + *-θra-), the shared semantics being 'cow-pen, herd' → 'stock, lineage' → 'inborn nature, essence' → 'gem'. So the material is right, the direction of the arrow is not, and the gloss 'substance' belongs to the Iranian branch, not to Vedic gotra-. Hence 'plausible' rather than 'confirmed'.",
